WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.346 --> 00:00:08.326
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.326 --> 00:00:10.306
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.306 --> 00:00:13.226
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.236 --> 00:00:15.006
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:15.006 --> 00:00:17.506
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:19.596
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.596 --> 00:00:21.456
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.986 --> 00:00:24.966
Today it is part two of our three-part

00:00:24.966 --> 00:00:28.426
mini-series on Tales from The Wild West.

00:00:28.956 --> 00:00:31.836
In part one, we looked at Wyatt Earp:

00:00:32.086 --> 00:00:34.576
perhaps the most famous lawman of the

00:00:34.586 --> 00:00:37.246
frontier, but one who wasn’t all that

00:00:37.246 --> 00:00:39.506
good at following the law himself.

00:00:40.176 --> 00:00:41.706
Today, we’re going to meet

00:00:41.706 --> 00:00:43.906
someone who almost certainly

00:00:44.046 --> 00:00:46.141
had a more extraordinary life.

00:00:46.626 --> 00:00:49.276
A man who made over three thousand

00:00:49.276 --> 00:00:51.426
arrests, who was never seriously

00:00:51.436 --> 00:00:53.966
wounded in the line of duty, who spoke

00:00:54.016 --> 00:00:56.746
multiple languages and used elaborate

00:00:56.766 --> 00:01:00.016
disguises to bring fugitives to justice.

00:01:00.386 --> 00:01:03.946
A man who was also almost entirely

00:01:03.966 --> 00:01:06.816
forgotten to history: Bass Reeves.

00:01:07.135 --> 00:01:10.076
It is an amazing story, so let’s not

00:01:10.076 --> 00:01:13.269
waste a minute, and get right into it.

00:01:15.647 --> 00:01:19.786
It is 1902, in Muskogee, in Oklahoma.

00:01:20.836 --> 00:01:23.796
A young man is wanted for murder.

00:01:24.636 --> 00:01:27.156
He has discovered that his wife

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:29.506
has been having an affair, and

00:01:29.786 --> 00:01:32.906
in a fit of rage, he pulls out

00:01:32.906 --> 00:01:35.526
his revolver and shoots her dead.

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:38.796
He turns the gun on himself, but

00:01:39.216 --> 00:01:42.216
somehow the bullet misses, only

00:01:42.216 --> 00:01:44.116
grazing the side of his head.

00:01:45.136 --> 00:01:48.676
He survives, and instead of turning

00:01:48.676 --> 00:01:51.896
himself in, he flees the murder scene.

00:01:53.226 --> 00:01:56.916
Unfortunately for him, Muskogee, Oklahoma

00:01:57.246 --> 00:02:00.246
is also the hunting ground of one of

00:02:00.246 --> 00:02:04.526
America’s most feared marshals, a man who

00:02:04.526 --> 00:02:07.846
has arrested thousands of criminals over a

00:02:07.876 --> 00:02:12.496
career spanning four decades: Bass Reeves.

00:02:13.406 --> 00:02:16.786
The young man, presumably, knows that

00:02:16.796 --> 00:02:19.236
the odds are stacked against him.

00:02:20.256 --> 00:02:23.436
The accounts of what happened next vary.

00:02:24.346 --> 00:02:27.426
Some have Bass Reeves following his

00:02:27.426 --> 00:02:30.316
target all the way into the wilderness,

00:02:30.666 --> 00:02:33.186
arresting him after a long chase.

00:02:33.766 --> 00:02:36.886
Others have Reeves tracking the man down

00:02:36.916 --> 00:02:41.006
at his home, and a tense standoff ensuing,

00:02:41.526 --> 00:02:44.166
with Reeves telling the man he can either

00:02:44.166 --> 00:02:47.546
give himself up, or he’ll be shot dead.

00:02:48.766 --> 00:02:51.166
Eventually, the man gives himself

00:02:51.166 --> 00:02:53.396
up, is taken into custody,

00:02:53.766 --> 00:02:55.796
is charged and sentenced.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.546
He escapes the death penalty and ends

00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:02.146
up serving eleven years in prison.

00:03:04.176 --> 00:03:07.596
For Bass Reeves, this man, Bennie,

00:03:08.006 --> 00:03:10.376
would be just one of the thousands

00:03:10.376 --> 00:03:13.676
of criminals he apprehended, and the

00:03:13.676 --> 00:03:16.356
story of how he was brought in is

00:03:16.476 --> 00:03:19.696
unremarkable apart from one detail:

00:03:20.606 --> 00:03:24.276
the man he arrested was his son.

00:03:25.706 --> 00:03:28.170
Now, we will return to this a

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.140
little later in the episode, but

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.030
first let me paint you a picture

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.210
of the early life of Bass Reeves.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.020
He was born in Crawford

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:42.831
County, Arkansas, in 1838.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.770
He was born into slavery,

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.300
and given the name Bass.

00:03:48.919 --> 00:03:52.400
Just: Bass, no surname, as slaves

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.240
in the American South weren’t

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:56.250
generally given family names.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.190
The name Reeves would come later,

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:01.270
from the family that owned him.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.770
His owner was a man named

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.450
William Steele Reeves.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.240
He was a farmer and

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.200
Arkansas state legislator.

00:04:11.731 --> 00:04:14.490
Bass’s early life is not well

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.080
documented, and what we know about

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.040
Reeves’s early years comes largely

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:22.330
from accounts he gave much later.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.496
There are three things worth pointing out.

00:04:27.168 --> 00:04:29.530
The first is that, from a young

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.330
age, he was physically exceptional.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.730
He was tall, 190 centimetres as a grown

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:39.960
man, and was extraordinarily strong.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.550
People were always talking about his

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.720
hands in particular: enormous hands,

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.700
and one of his signature moves was

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.670
to grab a criminal by the throat.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.640
The second is that he had an extraordinary

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:59.750
ability to read people: to watch, to

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.170
listen, to understand what someone

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:05.130
was thinking before they said it, to

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.750
figure out what someone’s next move

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:10.630
would be before they knew it themselves.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.300
And the final thing is that

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:16.580
he was an excellent shot.

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.690
This was unusual for slaves, as they

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:23.180
weren’t typically given access to guns

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.190
by their owners, for obvious reasons.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.440
Bass Reeves must have been trusted

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.750
enough, and he was trained with

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:34.740
firearms, primarily for hunting and

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.240
pest control on his master’s farm.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:41.250
He was so good that at local shooting

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.340
competitions, he was eventually

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:46.330
asked not to enter so that other

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.610
men had a fair chance of winning.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:53.240
He was unusual in his abilities, and this

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.950
meant that he was relieved of the more

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:59.090
arduous tasks that most slaves were made

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.590
to do, and instead became the “personal

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:05.970
servant” of his master’s son, George.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.470
He was still a slave, still the legal

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.120
property of another man, but his role

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:16.400
became more of a butler or personal

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.150
servant rather than a field labourer.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:23.420
And interestingly enough, one

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.290
account has it that he asked

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.710
his master, George Reeves, if he

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.380
could learn to read and write.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:34.670
George refused, but said that instead

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.470
he would teach him how to use weapons,

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.510
which perhaps hints at what was considered

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.210
more dangerous: a slave who could read

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.940
and write or a slave who could shoot.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:49.800
Now, when the Civil War broke out

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:53.950
in 1861, Bass went with his owner’s

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:56.160
son, George, who had been called up

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:57.870
to serve in the Confederate Army.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.050
Bass would have been in

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:01.880
his early 20s at the time.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.050
And at some point during the war, Bass and

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.210
George had a confrontation, an argument.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:14.460
The exact circumstances are unclear:

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.650
some accounts say it was over a

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:19.730
card game, some say it was over an

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:22.210
order that Reeves refused to follow.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:26.640
Whatever happened, Bass hit George.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:30.230
And in the Confederacy, for

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:33.070
a slave, striking a white man

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:35.720
was a potential death sentence.

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:38.570
Bass Reeves fled.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:41.970
He crossed into Indian Territory,

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:45.100
the area west of Arkansas that

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:46.840
is now the state of Oklahoma.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:49.980
And for those of you who aren’t

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:52.460
so familiar with the geography

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:54.710
of the US, we’re talking the

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.960
central, southern bit of the United

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.570
States, immediately above Texas.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:04.920
Now, Indian Territory in the 1860s was

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.340
unlike anywhere else in North America.

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:11.790
It had been established in the 1830s

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.730
as the destination for the so-called

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:17.940
Five Civilised Tribes, the Native

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:20.280
American groups who had been forcibly

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.570
removed from their homelands in the

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:25.220
American South in a catastrophic series

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:28.190
of events known as the Trail of Tears.

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:31.610
Tens of thousands of Native people

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:34.900
had been marched westward at gunpoint.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.810
Thousands had died on the journey.

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:41.250
This territory — Indian Territory

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.730
— this was what remained to them.

00:08:44.525 --> 00:08:47.645
It was a sort of ghetto, albeit a very

00:08:47.645 --> 00:08:50.105
large one in a very large country.

00:08:51.245 --> 00:08:53.475
And by the time Reeves

00:08:53.545 --> 00:08:55.755
arrived, it was a complex,

00:08:56.115 --> 00:08:58.845
multilingual, multicultural place.

00:08:59.495 --> 00:09:02.035
These “Indians”, these Native

00:09:02.035 --> 00:09:04.955
Americans, were not one, single people.

00:09:05.335 --> 00:09:07.935
The nations had their own governments,

00:09:07.935 --> 00:09:10.525
their own courts, their own laws.

00:09:11.735 --> 00:09:14.815
Bass Reeves settled among these people.

00:09:15.405 --> 00:09:17.935
He learned their languages, five of

00:09:17.935 --> 00:09:19.855
them, which he could speak fluently.

00:09:20.355 --> 00:09:21.065
He farmed.

00:09:21.775 --> 00:09:22.625
He built a life.

00:09:23.965 --> 00:09:26.135
During this time, he got to know the

00:09:26.135 --> 00:09:28.465
Territory like the back of his hand:

00:09:28.815 --> 00:09:31.675
the paths, the hiding places, the

00:09:31.675 --> 00:09:34.155
people, the feuds, and the networks.

00:09:34.475 --> 00:09:37.525
Which routes went where and who used them.

00:09:37.775 --> 00:09:39.725
Which families were connected.

00:09:40.135 --> 00:09:42.085
Who to trust and who not to.

00:09:42.655 --> 00:09:44.775
Who held grudges against who.

00:09:46.207 --> 00:09:49.997
In 1865, the Civil War ended, and

00:09:50.207 --> 00:09:52.647
with it came the Thirteenth Amendment

00:09:52.917 --> 00:09:54.577
and the abolition of slavery.

00:09:55.557 --> 00:09:58.057
Bass Reeves was a free man.

00:09:59.607 --> 00:10:01.797
He returned to Arkansas, and

00:10:01.797 --> 00:10:03.557
settled just over the border,

00:10:03.917 --> 00:10:05.727
in a town called Van Buren.

00:10:06.657 --> 00:10:09.567
He got married, set up his own farm, and

00:10:09.577 --> 00:10:12.507
had children, eventually eleven in total.

00:10:13.767 --> 00:10:17.057
For a decade or so, he lived quietly,

00:10:17.427 --> 00:10:19.147
minding his business, working

00:10:19.147 --> 00:10:21.287
his farm, raising his family.

00:10:22.437 --> 00:10:27.477
Then, in 1875, a man named Isaac Parker

00:10:27.877 --> 00:10:30.937
arrived in Fort Smith, Arkansas, to take

00:10:30.937 --> 00:10:33.857
up the position of federal judge for

00:10:33.867 --> 00:10:36.537
the Western District of Arkansas, the

00:10:36.537 --> 00:10:38.957
district that covered Indian Territory.

00:10:40.257 --> 00:10:43.927
Now, Parker was a stickler for justice,

00:10:44.117 --> 00:10:47.117
and was known as the Hanging Judge.

00:10:48.097 --> 00:10:49.587
In twenty-one years on the

00:10:49.587 --> 00:10:53.807
bench, he sentenced 160 men to

00:10:53.847 --> 00:10:57.137
death, 79 of whom were hanged.

00:10:57.837 --> 00:11:00.557
He was strict, moralistic, and

00:11:00.587 --> 00:11:02.947
completely convinced of his own

00:11:02.977 --> 00:11:05.167
righteousness, that he was doing

00:11:05.167 --> 00:11:07.587
the Lord and the State’s work.

00:11:08.817 --> 00:11:11.837
His belief was that Indian Territory was

00:11:11.857 --> 00:11:15.817
in dire need of order, and he was willing

00:11:15.817 --> 00:11:18.748
to be ruthless in his pursuit of it.

00:11:20.027 --> 00:11:22.607
And interestingly enough, one

00:11:22.647 --> 00:11:24.147
more progressive element of his

00:11:24.147 --> 00:11:27.177
worldview was that, compared to most

00:11:27.177 --> 00:11:29.727
men of his time, he was unusually

00:11:29.737 --> 00:11:32.587
willing to hire Black deputies.

00:11:34.267 --> 00:11:36.887
And as for the Territory itself,

00:11:37.237 --> 00:11:39.707
well, it was a pretty lawless place,

00:11:39.977 --> 00:11:42.907
and enforcing the law was a problem.

00:11:44.297 --> 00:11:46.727
For starters, it was vast.

00:11:47.037 --> 00:11:49.777
It covered just under 200,000 square

00:11:49.777 --> 00:11:52.957
kilometres, which is an area roughly the

00:11:52.957 --> 00:11:55.847
size of England and Wales put together.

00:11:57.377 --> 00:11:59.857
It had little formal internal

00:11:59.857 --> 00:12:01.467
law enforcement of its own.

00:12:02.347 --> 00:12:04.867
Fugitives from Kansas, Texas,

00:12:05.117 --> 00:12:07.397
Missouri, and Arkansas had learned

00:12:07.407 --> 00:12:09.637
that if you crossed into Indian

00:12:09.637 --> 00:12:13.087
Territory, most lawmen wouldn’t follow.

00:12:14.757 --> 00:12:16.917
Parker needed deputies

00:12:17.097 --> 00:12:18.707
who knew the Territory.

00:12:18.897 --> 00:12:20.077
Who knew the people.

00:12:20.107 --> 00:12:21.817
Who spoke the languages.

00:12:22.067 --> 00:12:24.117
Who could survive alone out there

00:12:24.337 --> 00:12:26.977
for days or weeks at a time.

00:12:28.597 --> 00:12:31.087
Bass Reeves fit that description

00:12:31.417 --> 00:12:33.597
better than almost anyone alive.

00:12:34.717 --> 00:12:37.977
In 1875, at the age of thirty-seven,

00:12:38.517 --> 00:12:41.017
Bass Reeves was appointed as a

00:12:41.027 --> 00:12:43.257
Deputy United States Marshal.

00:12:43.717 --> 00:12:46.337
He was one of, if not the first,

00:12:46.547 --> 00:12:48.797
Black men to hold that position

00:12:49.027 --> 00:12:50.697
west of the Mississippi River.

00:12:51.577 --> 00:12:53.597
It would be a position he would hold

00:12:53.597 --> 00:12:56.417
for thirty-two years, and it is not

00:12:56.467 --> 00:12:58.447
an overstatement to say that he had

00:12:58.447 --> 00:13:01.127
a legendary law enforcement career.

00:13:02.027 --> 00:13:04.697
He made over three thousand arrests.

00:13:05.087 --> 00:13:07.627
He killed a reported fourteen men

00:13:07.937 --> 00:13:10.277
in the line of duty, all of them,

00:13:10.427 --> 00:13:13.197
by every account, in self-defence.

00:13:14.887 --> 00:13:17.667
Amazingly enough, while hundreds of

00:13:17.667 --> 00:13:19.767
his fellow US marshals were being

00:13:19.797 --> 00:13:22.167
killed doing exactly the same job,

00:13:22.627 --> 00:13:24.847
he was never seriously injured.

00:13:25.607 --> 00:13:27.557
There is one account of him getting

00:13:27.557 --> 00:13:30.517
shot in the leg, but if this did

00:13:30.517 --> 00:13:32.747
happen, it didn’t seriously trouble him.

00:13:33.297 --> 00:13:35.337
He was going out and bringing in

00:13:35.337 --> 00:13:38.756
criminals right up into his late 60s.

00:13:40.147 --> 00:13:41.767
And he didn’t bring people

00:13:41.767 --> 00:13:43.727
in one at a time, either.

00:13:44.517 --> 00:13:46.677
On his best single trip, he

00:13:46.677 --> 00:13:49.387
brought in seventeen men, all

00:13:49.427 --> 00:13:51.987
chained together on an open wagon.

00:13:53.367 --> 00:13:55.497
He was paid for every criminal

00:13:55.497 --> 00:13:57.937
he brought in, and the fees

00:13:58.127 --> 00:13:59.637
could add up considerably.

00:14:00.847 --> 00:14:02.737
In the case of the seventeen-man

00:14:02.747 --> 00:14:05.887
haul, the fee he collected came to

00:14:05.907 --> 00:14:08.657
around nine hundred dollars, which is

00:14:08.667 --> 00:14:12.247
something like $35,000 in today’s money.

00:14:13.757 --> 00:14:16.347
And as to how he would go about the

00:14:16.347 --> 00:14:17.997
business of capturing criminals,

00:14:18.427 --> 00:14:20.637
well, there have been entire books

00:14:20.677 --> 00:14:23.237
written about these legendary arrests.

00:14:24.497 --> 00:14:26.747
His skin colour was an immediate

00:14:26.797 --> 00:14:29.947
advantage; lawmen tended to be white,

00:14:30.287 --> 00:14:32.947
so most outlaws simply wouldn’t

00:14:32.967 --> 00:14:35.447
expect a black man to arrest them.

00:14:36.427 --> 00:14:39.057
But as his reputation continued to

00:14:39.057 --> 00:14:41.377
grow, he had to find more and more

00:14:41.377 --> 00:14:43.547
ingenious ways of getting close

00:14:43.547 --> 00:14:45.617
enough to his target to arrest

00:14:45.617 --> 00:14:47.877
them without being shot at first.

00:14:48.917 --> 00:14:52.037
The key thing was disguising his identity,

00:14:52.427 --> 00:14:54.567
and the fact that he was an officer of

00:14:54.567 --> 00:14:57.257
the law, until the very last minute.

00:14:58.317 --> 00:15:00.797
He would sometimes ride into outlaw

00:15:00.827 --> 00:15:03.727
territory as a travelling preacher, his

00:15:03.727 --> 00:15:06.177
Bible open on the saddle in front of him.

00:15:07.217 --> 00:15:09.917
Wanted men would approach him for company,

00:15:10.317 --> 00:15:12.607
share his campfire, tell him things.

00:15:13.317 --> 00:15:15.187
He would make the arrest the following

00:15:15.187 --> 00:15:18.217
morning, or in some cases, the men would

00:15:18.217 --> 00:15:20.437
awake to find he had put the handcuffs

00:15:20.467 --> 00:15:22.357
on while they were still asleep.

00:15:23.597 --> 00:15:25.767
He went in as a farmer, as

00:15:25.767 --> 00:15:27.827
a drifter, as an old man.

00:15:28.747 --> 00:15:30.767
On at least one occasion, he

00:15:30.777 --> 00:15:33.277
dressed as a woman and walked into

00:15:33.277 --> 00:15:35.347
the camp of men he was seeking.

00:15:36.287 --> 00:15:39.337
He was a physically imposing man, 190

00:15:39.337 --> 00:15:41.677
centimetres tall, and very strong.

00:15:42.587 --> 00:15:44.637
Disguising this wasn’t easy.

00:15:44.947 --> 00:15:47.977
But Reeves had apparently learned a way

00:15:48.287 --> 00:15:51.367
to make himself seem considerably smaller

00:15:51.627 --> 00:15:54.217
on horseback, so he could approach his

00:15:54.217 --> 00:15:56.967
target without them realising who he was.

00:15:58.307 --> 00:16:01.237
He was a fantastic shot, deadly

00:16:01.237 --> 00:16:04.167
quick to pull out his gun but also

00:16:04.197 --> 00:16:06.837
incredibly accurate at long range.

00:16:07.537 --> 00:16:09.867
There is one account of him downing

00:16:09.877 --> 00:16:13.857
a target at a reported 450 metres.

00:16:14.547 --> 00:16:16.847
Now, that’s most likely a serious

00:16:16.847 --> 00:16:19.957
exaggeration, and there are question marks

00:16:20.167 --> 00:16:21.887
over whether his gun could even shoot

00:16:21.887 --> 00:16:24.887
that far, let alone hit a target, but

00:16:25.097 --> 00:16:27.437
it gives you an idea of his reputation.

00:16:29.257 --> 00:16:31.937
He was almost killed multiple times.

00:16:32.457 --> 00:16:35.337
Once, a pair of outlaws caught him.

00:16:36.117 --> 00:16:37.077
They had their guns

00:16:37.317 --> 00:16:38.587
pointed straight at him.

00:16:39.577 --> 00:16:41.547
Before killing him, they asked

00:16:41.857 --> 00:16:43.657
if he had any last words.

00:16:45.137 --> 00:16:47.667
Reeves said he had a letter from his

00:16:47.667 --> 00:16:50.177
wife in his pocket, and would very

00:16:50.177 --> 00:16:52.317
much like to read it before he died.

00:16:53.497 --> 00:16:55.117
He handed over the letter to

00:16:55.117 --> 00:16:57.397
his captors, his hand quivering.

00:16:58.527 --> 00:17:01.667
One took the letter, and when his

00:17:01.707 --> 00:17:04.757
eyes looked down, this gave Reeves the

00:17:04.757 --> 00:17:07.737
split second he needed to grab him by

00:17:07.757 --> 00:17:09.937
the throat and pull out his pistol.

00:17:11.295 --> 00:17:13.855
As it happens, Reeves couldn’t have

00:17:13.855 --> 00:17:16.335
read that letter even if he’d wanted to.

00:17:17.105 --> 00:17:19.115
Bass Reeves never learned to

00:17:19.115 --> 00:17:21.245
read or write, and was one of

00:17:21.245 --> 00:17:23.545
the few illiterate deputies.

00:17:24.485 --> 00:17:26.955
For most agents of the law, this

00:17:26.985 --> 00:17:29.095
would be a problem, as when you

00:17:29.105 --> 00:17:31.325
arrest someone, you need to read

00:17:31.325 --> 00:17:33.475
them the warrant for their arrest.

00:17:34.145 --> 00:17:37.165
But Reeves had an exceptional memory.

00:17:38.145 --> 00:17:40.095
When he received an arrest warrant

00:17:40.335 --> 00:17:42.535
for the first time, he would ask

00:17:42.535 --> 00:17:44.855
someone to read it aloud to him.

00:17:45.515 --> 00:17:47.495
He would look at the document, and

00:17:47.505 --> 00:17:50.035
remember which warrant referred to

00:17:50.035 --> 00:17:52.375
which person, through the shapes

00:17:52.375 --> 00:17:54.565
of certain letters or which warrant

00:17:54.595 --> 00:17:57.095
had a tear or a coffee stain on it.

00:17:58.225 --> 00:18:00.335
And when it came to the arrest, he

00:18:00.335 --> 00:18:02.225
would rifle through his satchel,

00:18:02.635 --> 00:18:04.985
find the appropriate warrant, and

00:18:05.005 --> 00:18:07.565
be able to recite it from memory.

00:18:09.005 --> 00:18:10.955
And there is one more detail.

00:18:11.595 --> 00:18:13.955
For much of his career, he worked

00:18:13.985 --> 00:18:16.185
alongside a Native American partner,

00:18:16.575 --> 00:18:18.545
someone who could move through the

00:18:18.545 --> 00:18:21.155
Territory even more easily than Reeves

00:18:21.155 --> 00:18:23.775
could, and who knew the land and the

00:18:23.775 --> 00:18:26.575
people in ways that even Reeves did not.

00:18:27.335 --> 00:18:28.825
Remember this, as it’ll

00:18:28.835 --> 00:18:29.925
be relevant in a minute.

00:18:31.405 --> 00:18:34.585
So, for almost thirty years, Bass

00:18:34.595 --> 00:18:36.855
Reeves had been doing exactly this:

00:18:37.385 --> 00:18:39.885
hunting dangerous fugitives through

00:18:39.915 --> 00:18:42.335
lawless territory, putting his life

00:18:42.375 --> 00:18:45.125
on the line almost every day, and

00:18:45.355 --> 00:18:48.115
miraculously, living to tell the tale.

00:18:49.405 --> 00:18:53.305
And then, in 1902, he heard the news that

00:18:53.305 --> 00:18:56.365
his son was on the list of wanted men.

00:18:57.695 --> 00:19:00.475
We have no record of why Bass

00:19:00.475 --> 00:19:02.185
Reeves volunteered himself to

00:19:02.185 --> 00:19:03.635
be the one to bring him in.

00:19:04.595 --> 00:19:07.445
To some people, it’s an example of

00:19:07.445 --> 00:19:09.965
his devout belief in law and order.

00:19:10.895 --> 00:19:13.745
To others, it’s more practical: he

00:19:13.745 --> 00:19:15.765
thought he had the best chance of

00:19:15.765 --> 00:19:18.895
saving his son’s life, and his son was

00:19:18.905 --> 00:19:21.595
more likely to accept his fate if it

00:19:21.595 --> 00:19:23.615
was his father that brought him in.

00:19:24.975 --> 00:19:27.805
Whatever the motivation, it worked.

00:19:28.285 --> 00:19:30.825
His son surrendered without a fight,

00:19:31.085 --> 00:19:33.715
escaped the death sentence, served eleven

00:19:33.715 --> 00:19:36.435
years in prison, and by all accounts,

00:19:36.565 --> 00:19:39.195
was a model citizen on his release.

00:19:40.405 --> 00:19:43.245
Still, Bass Reeves had arrested

00:19:43.455 --> 00:19:45.715
his own son and handed him over

00:19:45.715 --> 00:19:48.145
to a court he knew was highly

00:19:48.145 --> 00:19:50.285
likely to sentence him to death.

00:19:51.375 --> 00:19:54.375
He said nothing about it, by any account.

00:19:54.745 --> 00:19:56.325
He was just doing his job.

00:19:57.535 --> 00:19:59.495
It is the kind of thing that, were

00:19:59.495 --> 00:20:02.045
it done by a white man, no doubt

00:20:02.285 --> 00:20:03.975
would have been turned into a film.

00:20:04.615 --> 00:20:07.075
The great American lawman who will not

00:20:07.095 --> 00:20:09.995
bend the rules even for his own child.

00:20:10.325 --> 00:20:12.975
The integrity that costs everything.

00:20:14.105 --> 00:20:17.595
But it wasn’t made into a movie, and the

00:20:17.595 --> 00:20:21.505
story of Bass Reeves faded into obscurity.

00:20:22.905 --> 00:20:26.025
By the early 1900s, Indian Territory

00:20:26.185 --> 00:20:28.325
was being absorbed into what would

00:20:28.325 --> 00:20:30.105
become the state of Oklahoma.

00:20:30.795 --> 00:20:33.555
The districts that Reeves had patrolled

00:20:33.555 --> 00:20:36.615
for thirty years were being wound down.

00:20:37.355 --> 00:20:38.845
The Territory, as it

00:20:38.875 --> 00:20:40.895
had existed, was ending.

00:20:41.955 --> 00:20:44.595
Reeves was not a young man any more.

00:20:45.395 --> 00:20:47.225
But he did not retire.

00:20:47.845 --> 00:20:50.635
He took a job as a city police officer

00:20:50.845 --> 00:20:53.725
in Muskogee and carried on working

00:20:54.005 --> 00:20:56.225
until he had to stop for health reasons.

00:20:57.215 --> 00:21:01.170
He died in 1910, at the age of 71.

00:21:02.045 --> 00:21:03.885
It was not marked with great

00:21:03.885 --> 00:21:06.605
ceremony, no great obituary in

00:21:06.605 --> 00:21:08.595
the newspaper or large funeral.

00:21:09.725 --> 00:21:12.955
Indeed, to this day, nobody

00:21:12.955 --> 00:21:14.615
knows where he is buried.

00:21:16.195 --> 00:21:18.835
And as for why he was simply

00:21:18.845 --> 00:21:21.475
forgotten, well, the simplest

00:21:21.475 --> 00:21:23.535
explanation is that he didn’t fit

00:21:23.565 --> 00:21:25.875
the mould of the Wild West hero.

00:21:26.875 --> 00:21:29.065
The myth of the West was

00:21:29.065 --> 00:21:30.585
built on white heroes.

00:21:30.825 --> 00:21:33.345
People like Wyatt Earp, who you

00:21:33.345 --> 00:21:34.875
heard about in the last episode,

00:21:35.195 --> 00:21:37.475
and who certainly had a less

00:21:37.495 --> 00:21:39.555
heroic life than Bass Reeves.

00:21:40.705 --> 00:21:43.155
As a black man, Reeves’s story

00:21:43.445 --> 00:21:45.085
was destined to be forgotten.

00:21:46.385 --> 00:21:49.185
There is one theory, however, that the

00:21:49.185 --> 00:21:53.395
story of Bass Reeves is widely known, just

00:21:53.665 --> 00:21:56.995
under a different name: The Lone Ranger.

00:21:58.295 --> 00:22:00.765
Now, this was a radio programme

00:22:01.065 --> 00:22:04.615
first broadcast in 1933 about a

00:22:04.615 --> 00:22:07.485
fictional masked hero who has a native

00:22:07.515 --> 00:22:09.705
American companion called Tonto.

00:22:10.495 --> 00:22:11.795
He was a crack shot.

00:22:12.435 --> 00:22:13.235
He was alone.

00:22:13.435 --> 00:22:14.575
He was mysterious.

00:22:15.015 --> 00:22:17.015
He had a horse called Silver.

00:22:18.255 --> 00:22:20.515
The parallels with Bass Reeves

00:22:20.755 --> 00:22:22.795
are striking enough that several

00:22:22.795 --> 00:22:25.505
historians believe Reeves was the

00:22:25.535 --> 00:22:27.635
direct inspiration for the character.

00:22:29.055 --> 00:22:31.885
This is disputed, I should add; many

00:22:31.885 --> 00:22:33.985
lawmen worked with Native American

00:22:34.015 --> 00:22:36.855
companions, and wearing disguises and

00:22:36.855 --> 00:22:39.655
being a crack shot could equally be

00:22:39.695 --> 00:22:42.435
elements of any Western story rather

00:22:42.665 --> 00:22:45.255
than being directly inspired by Reeves.

00:22:46.365 --> 00:22:49.965
What can’t be disputed is that if the Lone

00:22:49.965 --> 00:22:53.115
Ranger was inspired by Bass Reeves, the

00:22:53.115 --> 00:22:56.405
protagonist needed to become white to be

00:22:56.405 --> 00:22:58.955
acceptable to a mass American audience.

00:22:59.785 --> 00:23:01.945
The Native American companion

00:23:01.945 --> 00:23:03.965
stayed, as did the skill with a

00:23:03.965 --> 00:23:06.585
gun and the penchant for disguise.

00:23:07.245 --> 00:23:10.265
The heroic agency, the man doing

00:23:10.265 --> 00:23:12.205
the arresting, the man taking all

00:23:12.205 --> 00:23:14.595
the risks, was simply transferred

00:23:14.905 --> 00:23:17.375
from a Black man to a white one.

00:23:18.565 --> 00:23:21.245
So, to wrap things up, the Wild

00:23:21.245 --> 00:23:23.165
West of popular imagination

00:23:23.405 --> 00:23:25.505
is a story about white men.

00:23:26.245 --> 00:23:28.975
Cowboys and outlaws and sheriffs.

00:23:29.455 --> 00:23:31.575
That story is real in the

00:23:31.575 --> 00:23:33.365
sense that those men existed.

00:23:33.875 --> 00:23:36.265
But the actual West was

00:23:36.265 --> 00:23:38.095
a far more mixed place.

00:23:38.445 --> 00:23:40.575
There were black cowboys and black

00:23:40.615 --> 00:23:43.145
lawmen, Chinese labourers who built

00:23:43.175 --> 00:23:44.975
much of the railway network, the

00:23:45.175 --> 00:23:47.555
Native and Mexican communities who were

00:23:47.555 --> 00:23:49.745
there before everyone else arrived.

00:23:50.515 --> 00:23:52.955
The myth of the Wild West, and the

00:23:53.125 --> 00:23:55.465
Hollywood version, was built by

00:23:55.485 --> 00:23:57.995
selecting some of those people and

00:23:58.015 --> 00:24:00.195
conveniently ignoring the rest.

00:24:01.155 --> 00:24:03.585
Someone like Bass Reeves lived

00:24:03.625 --> 00:24:05.835
an exceptional life yet was

00:24:05.875 --> 00:24:07.355
almost forgotten to history.

00:24:08.105 --> 00:24:10.415
He spent thirty-two years doing the

00:24:10.415 --> 00:24:13.475
hardest law enforcement work in America.

00:24:13.875 --> 00:24:15.735
He made three thousand arrests.

00:24:16.065 --> 00:24:18.155
He brought his own son to trial.

00:24:18.585 --> 00:24:20.395
He never bent the rules.

00:24:20.945 --> 00:24:23.555
He just happened to be the wrong colour.

00:24:25.185 --> 00:24:27.055
OK then, that is it for

00:24:27.055 --> 00:24:29.525
today’s episode on Bass Reeves.

00:24:29.845 --> 00:24:31.705
As a reminder, this was part

00:24:31.725 --> 00:24:34.055
two of a three-part mini-series

00:24:34.225 --> 00:24:36.225
on stories from the Wild West.

00:24:36.515 --> 00:24:37.535
In case you missed part

00:24:37.535 --> 00:24:39.525
one, it was on Wyatt Earp.

00:24:39.755 --> 00:24:42.315
And next up, in part three, we’ll look

00:24:42.315 --> 00:24:44.105
at the Wild West from a completely

00:24:44.105 --> 00:24:46.315
different angle: through the story of

00:24:46.355 --> 00:24:48.635
Geronimo, the Native American leader.

00:24:49.625 --> 00:24:51.515
You’ve been listening to English Learning

00:24:51.515 --> 00:24:54.335
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:54.695 --> 00:24:57.475
I’m Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:57.475 --> 00:24:59.705
I’ll catch you in the next episode.

